english_question:
The three views of a pyramid are shown in the figure. What is the surface area of this pyramid (unit: c $m^{2}$)?
A. 48 + 12 \sqrt{2}
B. 48 + 24 \sqrt{2}
C. 36 + 12 \sqrt{2}
D. 36 + 24 \sqrt{2}

As shown in the figure, a square ABCD with a side length of 1 rotates counterclockwise by 30° around point A to become square AB'C'D'. The area of the shaded part is ( )
A. 1 - \frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}
B. \frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}
C. 1 - \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}
D. 1

As shown in the figure, the side length of square paper ABCD is 2. Fold angles B and D so that the two right-angle vertices coincide at point P on diagonal BD. EF and GH are the crease lines. Let AE = x (0 < x < 2). Evaluate the truth of the following four statements: 
1. When x = 1, point P is the center of square ABCD; 
2. When x = 1 or 2, EF + GH > AC; 
3. When 0 < x < 2, the maximum area of hexagon AEFCHG is 3; 
4. When 0 < x < 2, the perimeter of hexagon AEFCHG remains unchanged. 
Choose the correct option from A, B, C, D.

The three views of a certain geometric body are shown in question (6). Its side view is an equilateral triangle with a side length of 1, and its top view is a rhombus formed by two equilateral triangles. What is the volume of this geometric body?
A. 1
B. \frac{1}{2}
C. \frac{1}{4}
D. \frac{1}{8}

The right figure is a visual representation of a triangular pyramid and its three views, all of which are right triangles. Then, b = ( )
A. 2
B. \sqrt { 3 }
C. \sqrt { 5 }
D. \sqrt { 6 }
